    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/lecturerphd-researcher-private-law-liability-position-european-ports": {
      "title": "Lecturer/(PhD) researcher private-law liability position of European ports",
      "description": "Lecturer/(PhD) researcher private-law liability position of European ports | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Lecturer/(PhD) researcher private-law liability position of European ports Listen Published Monday 14 Sep 2026 Deadline Sunday 25 Oct 2026 Work area Academic staff Organisational unit Erasmus School of Law (ESL) Salary € 3.706 - € 5.760 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Introduction Erasmus School of Law (ESL) is looking for a talented and motivated researcher and lecturer for a six-year lecturer/researcher position within the Department of Law & Markets. The candidate will combine teaching tasks (60%) with PhD research (40%) into the private-law liability position of European ports. The research time of 40% is protected and is intended for the completion of the PhD project described below. The intended starting date is 1 January 2027. Job description The teaching component falls within the broad scope of Law & Markets and will be determined on the basis of the candidate's expertise and the department's teaching needs. Initially, a substantial part of the teaching is expected to be in the master’s programme in Commercial Law. Indicatively, the teaching duties will focus on a limited, coherent set of courses and may include tutorials, assessment and thesis supervision, with guidance from experienced colleagues. Responsibilities will be tailored to the candidate's experience and stage of development; independent course coordination and substantial teaching development will not be expected at the outset. The precise allocation may vary from year to year in consultation with the department, while the agreed balance between teaching and research will be maintained. PhD research The PhD research concerns the private-law liability position of European sea and inland ports in the digital transition. Ports increasingly operate at the intersection of private enterprise, public infrastructure and European regulation. They are subject to expanding regulatory and statutory obligations relating to cybersecurity, digitalisation, critical infrastructure, data governance and sustainability. These obligations cannot always be classified neatly as either public or private law. Some of them are implemented in private relationships, for example through contractual arrangements concerning supply-chain security. Ports also organise many of their activities through private-law instruments, including access agreements, terminal contracts, data-sharing arrangements and contractual limitations of liability. The central question is how these obligations operate in private relationships and affect private-law duties of care, standards of liability, enforcement and the contractual allocation or limitation of risk. The research will examine, among other matters, the relevance of the EU Port Services Regulation, the NIS2 framework and related European and national rules on cybersecurity and critical infrastructure, including the Dutch Cyberbeveiligingswet. Particular attention will be paid to the extent to which regulatory duties shape open private-law standards and contractual relationships, and to the legal effectiveness of provisions that seek to allocate risks arising from digital incidents. The project will cover both sea and inland ports and will use selected European jurisdictions and ports for comparison. It will also consider whether, and if so how, the frequent public ownership of port companies affects the relationship between public and private law and the resulting liability position. The principal method will be doctrinal legal research at the intersection of private law, commercial and maritime law, public law and EU law, supplemented by a functional comparative analysis. If useful and feasible, the legal analysis may be informed by interviews with relevant stakeholders, subject to the applicable research-integrity and privacy requirements. The project primarily contributes to ESL's research priority Digitalisation. Within this thematic priority, it brings together private-law, maritime-law and public-law expertise to examine how digital port infrastructure and data-driven operations reshape duties of care and contractual risk allocation. Its examination of cybersecurity, critical infrastructure and the legal allocation of digital risk also connects with the priority Security, rule of law and social resilience. The proposed supervisory team consists of Prof. Jolien Kruit as first supervisor, Dr Frank Stevens as co-supervisor, and Prof. Pim Jansen as second supervisor with particular responsibility for the interface between public and private law. Job requirements We are looking for a candidate who: has completed, or is close to completing, an LL.M. or equivalent master's degree in law with a strong academic record; demonstrates clear potential for academic research, preferably supported by prior research experience or a substantial thesis; has a strong interest in private law, commercial law, maritime or transport law and is able to connect these fields with EU law, public law and digital regulation; has the attitude and motivation to teach and supervise students within the department of Law & Markets; is proficient in written and spoken English (C1 level); is proficient in written and spoken Dutch. Candidates who have at least B2 level in Dutch at the moment of application and are willing and able to achieve the level of Dutch required for the relevant teaching duties within the first year of the PhD for relevant teaching duties are also welcome to apply; and works independently, accurately and collegially and is able to combine long-term research with teaching responsibilities. Students who are currently completing their master's degree may apply, provided that the degree is obtained no later than three weeks before the intended starting date. Candidates who already hold a PhD or who are currently preparing a PhD dissertation elsewhere are generally not eligible for this position. Employment conditions and benefits The position is in principle for a period of 6 years, starting 1 January 2027. It has a teaching component of 60%, next to a research component of 40%. The gross monthly salary (scale 10, CAO-NU) is between € 3.706 and € 5.760. You will be based at Erasmus School of Law, Department Law & Markets in Rotterdam. The lecturer/researcher will also become part of Erasmus Graduate School of Law. The position starts with a temporary employment contract for 18 months. This probationary period consists of an educational programme, offered by Erasmus Graduate School of Law, individual research and teaching tasks. After one year, the progress will be evaluated. In case of a positive evaluation, the contract will be extended. In both phases structured guidance is provided by the thesis supervisors and Erasmus Graduate School of Law. Next to that, we offer you: Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 29 days of paid leave with a 38-hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre ! Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR , FAME , QuEUR, or Young Erasmus Academy . Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: d evelopment days and a personal career budget , foreign exchange opportunities with most costs paid by EUR, and free access to our university library . Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare . Erasmus University Rotterdam aspires to be an equitable and inclusive community. We nurture an open culture, where everyone is supported to fulfill their full potential. We see inclusivity of talent as the basis of our successes, and the diversity of perspectives and people as a highly valued outcome. EUR provides equal opportunities to all employees and applicants regardless of gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, ethnicity, age, neurodiversity, functional impairment, citizenship, or any other aspect which makes them unique. We look forward to welcoming you to our community. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service Department Law & Markets The selected candidate will be embedded within the Law & Markets department, home of a vibrant and diverse academic community. The overarching mission of the Department Law & Markets is to develop and deliver cutting-edge, high-quality research and teaching on legal institutions shaping markets and their implications for socio-economic justice and sustainability. The research group specifically studies legal and societal problems generated in the complex dynamics of international, European and domestic laws regulating technology, trade, industry, investment, money, and more generally markets. The focus is on how to make the law in any form socially, environmentally and financially sustainable, how to deal with technological innovation on an inclusive basis and how to combine private and public interests responsibly. Researchers have different profiles, including law and technology, public law, international and EU law, legal theory, and commercial law. The Department is keen on multidisciplinary research. The Department is responsible for a considerable part of the bachelor and master curricula of Erasmus School of Law. Specifically, Law & Markets provides bachelor teaching in constitutional and administrative law, international and European Union law, jurisprudence, legal philosophy and commercial law. Furthermore, Law & Markets is responsible for several master programmes including in law and technology public law, international and European Union law and commercial law. Your teaching activities will take place within these programmes and courses; the particular teaching tasks will be decided on the basis of the expertise of the candidate as well as the needs of the Department. Teaching tasks for bachelor courses will be mostly in Dutch. Teaching in English is required in the Master Programmes and may also be part of the tasks. Erasmus School of Law Erasmus School of Law employs 500 members of staff and is attended by around 5000 students. Erasmus School of Law offers bachelor programmes in Law, Tax Law and Criminology. Next to that, Erasmus School of Law offers a wide variety of master programmes and several postgraduate tracks. At Erasmus School of Law, the fundamental premise of academic research is that law cannot be considered in complete isolation or as an end in itself. It is embedded in an economic and social context that shapes law. At the same time, law shapes society and defines economic relationships. In line with this vision, the mission of Erasmus School of Law is to carry out innovative research on the function of law in its economic and social context. The overarching theme of Erasmus School of Law is therefore 'Where law meets business': Erasmus School of Law is all about the interplay between law, practice and society. Both research and teaching at Erasmus School of Law have a strong social and business orientation. Erasmus School of Law is committed to promoting international and interdisciplinary research, as evidenced by its participation in various international research collaborations. Additional information More information about Erasmus Universiteit Rotterdam , Erasmus School of Law and Erasmus Graduate School of Law can be found on the websites. Additional information about the vacancy can be requested by contacting the Erasmus Graduate School of Law office via egsl@law.eur.nl or via one of the supervisors (kruit@vantraa.nl ; f.stevens@law.eur.nl ; jansen@law.eur.nl ). Applying Deadline for application is 25 October 2026. Applicants are invited to submit a set of application files (in English): EGSL application form including a research proposal (max 2000 words), merged in 1 document, motivation letter (max 2 pages), CV, copy Master’s diploma and transcripts (or information on the expected graduation date), and, where applicable, evidence of English-language proficiency. The application form and more information can be found on the EGSL-website . Please apply via the Erasmus University’s application portal. Interviews are expected to take place on 4 or 5 November 2026 . Please be aware that invitations for interviews will be send a very short time before, so make sure you are available these dates. If there are days or times within this timeframe that you are not available, please inform EGSL ( egsl@law.eur.nl ) about this before the application deadline. We will try to take this into account. Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Lecturer/(PhD) researcher private-law liability position of European ports Close Interested? Interested? Compare @count study programme Close Compare @count study programme @title Duration: @duration Remove @title Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Lecturer/(PhD) researcher private-law liability position of European ports Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
